{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511904-what-s-your-vision-of-humanity","id":1511904,"title":"What's your vision of Humanity?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-05-17T13:04:29-07:00","id":1511904,"title":"What's your vision of Humanity?","content":"In the sense of how harsh are you with asking for Conscience checks to not lose Humanity. I know it varies a lot from one Storyteller to another, I know some that consider that at certain low levels of Humanity, not even diablerie counts as a sin, what I think is problematic. This thread originated mostly because of the book <b><i>Lair of the Hidden</i></b>. It's one of the books I like the most in Revised era and I have read it many times and always find myself coming back to it, there's one thing always bothers me. It's the fact that the leaders of the <b>Inconnu</b>, the <b>Twelve</b> in the case, all take part in a ritual that involves the sacrifice of humans and yet most of them have somewhat high levels of Humanity on their character sheets. Even though I usually refrain myself from the more manichaeistic visions of the world, both in real life and certainly even more so in the WoD, I think that the fact the Inconnu accepts the idea of practicing something that is regarded as one of the most monstrous things someone can do and still retain a somewhat high level of humanity is startling.<br /> <br /> So, I ask here again in search of perhaps finding some different views, how do you treat Humanity and Sin in your games?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12348,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","user":"Herr Meister","date":". 05-17-2023, 06:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T13:54:09-07:00","id":1511911,"title":"","content":"I generally find most players have a sweet spot of where they want their PC's Humanity to be (usually between 4-7). That is either based on their favored roleplay style, or what they think their Humanity needs to be in order to accomplish their goals (unscrupulous empire builders tend towards the lower Humanity ranks). Going below that rank matters to them. They don't care if they start higher and descend to it.<br /> <br /> So regardless of where their Humanity starts out as, I ask the players before the chronicle begins if they 1) want to roll their Conscience every time they violate things - knowing they're driving their character to a lower Humanity anyway - as they like roll playing out remorse, 2) if they want to concede the loss of Humanity (and thus make no dice rolls) after certain kinds of violations because such roleplay only gets in their way of the kind of character they want to play, or 3) do they simply want to start at a lower of Humanity they are more comfortable with? <br /> <br /> It is usually some 2 or 3 or some combination. Only those players who want to try playing a moral character are fine with going with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/node/1\" >Home</a><br /> <br /> In my experience, most players only want to make Conscience rolls and feel remorse for their actions if that is something they want to roleplay for that specific PC. That may be because they want to play a moral character struggling with their vampirism, or because it finally involves actions they want to be rare and want to prevent future Humanity loss. Players of unscrupulous businessmen may care about planned murder or even impassioned violence or killing in self-defense and want to roll when that happens, but don't care about theft, extortion, property damage, threats, etc. to build their financial empire. Rolling Conscience and having to roleplay out remorse in those conditions just isn't fun or worthwhile to them. They'd rather just be a ruthless bastard and take the hit, an reserve those rolls for when it matters to them.<br /> <br /> The issue is whether they think making rolls will disrupt or slow down the game, or if they feel their ruthless businessman feeling remorse for ruining a business rival would be false. They only want to make the rolls when it matters for them. So reducing the number of rolls until it reaches territory that really matters for them, has been an idea they have welcomed.<br /> <br /> So we try to only roll when it truly matters for the characters (and that is different for each PC), and reduce the total number of dice rolling in general.<br /> <br /> In terms of Humanity (or Path ratings) of NPCs, this has always been something that VtM sourcebooks struggle with. Character descriptions often don't match their Path ratings. Revised did seem to do a better job overall than 1e/2e (CbN's Humanity ranks are notoriously non-sensical), but they still have plenty of misfires. Either adjust accordingly, or figure out a way to justify it that makes sense to you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T14:04:41-07:00","id":1511912,"title":"","content":"Humanity is one of those things that you almost have to go off of the individual character rather than a default mechanic.<br /> <br /> I have had games where one PC is more or less an anime vampire idol singer, whose idea of betrayal is being mean to her fans or cutting her loyal agent out of a bonus because he is blood bonded and wouldn't complain. On the other hand in the same group was a noire style detective character who was inspired by old Sam Spade bogart movies but leaned heavily on the max payne body count method of problem solving. Both were on the path of humanity, but they had wildly different priorities when it came to what was important when it came to what constituted a line they would not cross.<br /> <br /> Just looking at the V20 path charts, most of the players I gamed with didn't fit the humanity path IRL, their characters only went further off the beaten track as far as morality went.<br /> <br /> I usually sit down with the players and get a sense of the morality zone they are wanting the character to work within, during play I keep them inside that framework, but will work with them when they want to take the character in a different direction or feel the character needs an emotional arc of sorts. Yes this turns the game mechanic from something that is a dice roll to a Player/ST collaboration but that tended to work out better for my group's needs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T14:26:45-07:00","id":1511914,"title":"","content":"To be fair, there's no technical or lore issue with the twelve having high humanity while sacrificing a human per year for centuries.<br /> <br /> Firstly because doing a sin against humanity doesn't immediatly lower it. You have to fail at feeling remorse.<br /> <br /> And from what I gather from the book, the twelve, or at least a lot of them, are indeed remorseful about the yearly killing, seeing it as a painful, yet necessary action.<br /> <br /> Plus, even when they would fail to feel remorse and hence lose some humanity, it can be regained. Which in universe means one seeks to be better. And as the twelve desires to walk saulot's path, they will rise their humanity.<br /> <br /> <br /> In game terms, this would manifest as the twelve generally succeeding their conscience roll each year, or they regain their lost humanity point during said year with obsessive introspection and study of saulot's way.<br /> <br /> <br /> I had a character like that once. An assamite whobwas a hitman in life. Desiring to leave behind his murderous ways, but trapped by his duties as assassin for his sire and clan.<br /> <br /> And yet, he was quasi perpetually at humanity 6 or above. Because he had a high conscience rating (which I rationalized as representing his deep desire to be better), so he often kept his humanity's ratting despite cold blood murders most of the time by feeling remorse, and when he did lose a point, I bought it back with XP, and justified it in game as him being horrified at his lack of feelings for a specific assassination target, hence going on an introspection.<br /> <br /> <br /> Part of the misunderstansding, I think, is that people sees humanity as a mesure of one's actions, while it's in fact a mesure of one's reaction to theirs actions.<br /> <br /> Both a humanity 9 and a humanity 1 vampires can plan mass murders, the difference is that one will be remorsefull while the other will not care","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24043,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","user":"Nyremne","date":". 05-17-2023, 05:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T14:27:16-07:00","id":1511915,"title":"","content":"Duplicate...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","user":"Shadeprowler","date":". 05-18-2023, 02:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T14:35:28-07:00","id":1511917,"title":"","content":"Damn it, I edited my post and went to spamwile... Starting to HATE the forum... one more time now<br /> Will delet ethe original when appear... <br /> <br /> In my humble opinion, there is no such thing as &quot;universal Humanity&quot; or good or evil<br /> In Vampire Humanity is a measure of how closely a vampire clings to the morality and values of his or her mortal life (and consequently how well they are able to resist the Beast - and the Beast iself is not necesarily &quot;evil&quot; , tho' if you consider the Beast a primitive P'o from Kindred of the East or the Shadow from Wraith, then it is... sort of. But the Beast is the inner predator whos main goal is to survive. It is like the Alien Xenomorph (the Alien is not evil, just.. what it is) brutal, effective perfect predator, dangerous, wants to eat, wants to survive and unless you picked the Flaw and your Beast is sentinent &quot;alternate personality *cough* P'o *cough* Shadow *cough* it is not very intelligent.)<br /> Morality and values are not set in stone. They are affected by many things, your relegion, your family, your upbringing, society and most importantly the time period you are from<br /> It is totally possible you have very different morality and values thyn your grandparents or great grand parents; to the point you even could consider them... evil by modern or your standards<br /> Public execution? Shaming? Other jolly old habits from ~100 years or more ago? Totally normal. Not something evil. Or amoral. Well by the standards of that time period.<br /> Every Kindred was a Human before their Embrace... and if anything the past 10.000 years thought us? Well Humans are... individulasl. And generally horrible.<br /> We always find a reason or excuse to be &quot;not nice&quot;.<br /> So when I consider Humanity and when my players create their character, the first thing I ask them to define those things; when, where they were born, their social or relegious upbringing, and so on, in short: I ask them to define their Morality and values - what made them, shaped them to the person they were before the Embrace and those answers will be the basic framework how I will treat their Humanity<br /> <br /> As for the Twelve?<br /> The are Old.<br /> <b>Danika Ruthven</b> was born in the 1st Century, her Humanity is 6 - She was unwilling to surrender her home to the Usurpers. Since then she has lost her interest in Golconda, focusing solely on preserving her domain and continuing to be a good hostess<br /> <b>Abdalkutba</b> was Embraced in the late 1st century, rociking a Humanity of 5 - After consulting the stars, he agreed to join in the Pact, as he had learned that the demon who Cretheus planned to summon was one of the beings that first brought light into the cosmos and had seen God in person<br /> <b>Cretheus </b>was born circa 465 BCE with a whooping Humanity of 3<br /> <b>Demetrius of Antioch</b> was Embraced in 320 BCE have a Humanity of 8 but insane; he joined the pact willingly (although naively), thinking it to be for the greater good. Once the pact was joined, the demon all but suppressed Democritus' Beast and the fact that his vampiric derangement also makes him labour under the delusion he doesn't feed (he does, he just doesn't remember it afterward, and usually neither does his vessel), makes for a very compelling ruse. In his insanity made an alternate personality, the &quot;evil&quot; Master of Ravens<br /> <b>Drenis </b>was Embraced in the late 2nd century and while have a Humanity of 7 she accepts human sacrifice as a fact of life<br /> <b>Eshmunamash</b> was Embraced in the 16th century B.C. with Humanity of 6<br /> <b>\u200bHilderic</b> was Embraced in circa 340 CE with Humanity of 4<br /> <b>Pentweret </b>was Embraced180 BCE, with Humanity of 5, he joined the Pact because he thought that it would be a test for one's own morals in the path to Golconda<br /> <b>Mahtiel </b>is not only from the 3rd century AD but not even on Humanity but on Road of Kings with 6<br /> <br /> These numbers are... not that high actually<br /> And their age, the period they are from, thus their Morality and values are not quite rigid when it comes to those things like human sacrifice.<br /> Lets not forget, many traditions of Abrahamic religions such as Judaism, Christianity and Islam consider that God commanded Abraham to sacrifice his son to examine obedience of Abraham to His commands. To prove his obedience, Abraham intended to sacrifice his son (However at the eleventh hour God commanded Abraham to sacrifice a ram instead of his son... but still)<br /> Christianity developed the belief that the story of Isaac's binding was a foreshadowing of the sacrifice of Christ, whose death and resurrection enabled the salvation and atonement for man from its sins, including original sin.\u200b\u200b<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T15:43:43-07:00","id":1511930,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In my experience, most players only want to make Conscience rolls and feel remorse for their actions if that is something they want to roleplay for that specific PC. </div> </div> </div>That's very interesting, by the way I deal similarly with my players, but always wonder how other people (Storytellers) handle such things. The case you mentioned often happens, I'd say. Players only want to &quot;regret their actions&quot; if they think it was unjustified, so the conscience rolls themselves many times end up being redundant. I really liked your idea of talking with the players to see how we adjust this kind of thing, sometimes it's really better to just make the Humanity drop when it makes sense and the character wouldn't regret their actions.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In terms of Humanity (or Path ratings) of NPCs, this has always been something that VtM sourcebooks struggle with. Character descriptions often don't match their Path ratings. Revised did seem to do a better job overall than 1e/2e (CbN's Humanity ranks are notoriously non-sensical), but they still have plenty of misfires. </div> </div> </div>This, if I'm not mistaken, Menele had Humanity 10, something that makes absolutely no sense for a character like him, even when we take into consideration the points brought up here by the others.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Humanity is one of those things that you almost have to go off of the individual character rather than a default mechanic. </div> </div> </div>True, it's hard to imagine any kind of &quot;universal code of behavior&quot;, the closer we could get is Kant's <i>Categorical Imperative</i>, still one could argue that it was influenced by Christian morality, but for me such affirmation makes no sense.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Both a humanity 9 and a humanity 1 vampires can plan mass murders, the difference is that one will be remorsefull while the other will not care </div> </div> </div>I really disagree with this statement. I think one of the main ideas of a Humanity rating is to determine how far/close a vampire is with the <i>Beast</i>, so, in a way, it is affected by AND affects the behavior of the Cainite in question, I think mass murder/genocide would never cross the mind of a Cainite (or a human being, for the matter) with humanity 9.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In my humble opinion, there is no such thing as &quot;universal Humanity&quot; or good or evil<br /> In Vampire Humanity is a measure of how closely a vampire clings to the morality and values of his or her mortal life (and consequently how well they are able to resist the Beast - and the Beast iself is not necesarily &quot;evil&quot; , tho' if you consider the Beast a primitive P'o from Kindred of the East or the Shadow from Wraith, then it is... sort of. But the Beast is the inner predator whos main goal is to survive. It is like the Alien Xenomorph (the Alien is not evil, just.. what it is) brutal, effective perfect predator, dangerous, wants to eat, wants to survive and unless you picked the Flaw and your Beast is sentinent &quot;alternate personality *cough* P'o *cough* Shadow *cough* it is not very intelligent.)\u200b </div> </div> </div>I agree, it's naturally hard to find a &quot;<i>Golden Middle&quot;</i> in matters of morality when one takes into consideration the various changes in society and worldview that happened throughout history, <b>but</b> there are things that are universally viewed as monstrous, or there are not? I think it's really hard to find a &quot;point of compromise&quot; regarding humanity, I mean, if it (as the other Paths, for the matter) is only seen as a way to measure one's own view of one's actions, as in a way to justify his actions for himself, etc, thent one could find a way to justify basically anything and think he's doing the right thing, so that the <i>Beast </i>wouldn't take a &quot;grow&quot; inside him. It's not hard for some human being, let alone Cainites, to think that &quot;the ends justify the means&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T15:54:10-07:00","id":1511933,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> These numbers are... not that high actually<br /> And their age, the period they are from, thus their Morality and values are not quite rigid when it comes to those things like human sacrifice.<br /> Lets not forget, many traditions of Abrahamic religions such as Judaism, Christianity and Islam consider that God commanded Abraham to sacrifice his son to examine obedience of Abraham to His commands. To prove his obedience, Abraham intended to sacrifice his son (However at the eleventh hour God commanded Abraham to sacrifice a ram instead of his son... but still)<br /> Christianity developed the belief that the story of Isaac's binding was a foreshadowing of the sacrifice of Christ, whose death and resurrection enabled the salvation and atonement for man from its sins, including original sin.\u200b\u200b\u200b </div> </div> </div>This is actually a fair point, but then again they're not sacrificing people to God, but to a demon. Regarding the numbers, they are really not that high, in any case not low as well, except for Cretheus, who in my opinion doesn't really pursue Golconda, but something more akin to Apotheosis. You brought up two other interesting ideas, one is the question of the Beast being &quot;bad&quot;, the way I see it, the answer would most likely be &quot;yes&quot;, it only cares for itself and is a destructive force altogether. Another point if how &quot;intelligent&quot; is the Beast, this is something that often becomes the subject of discussion with questions like: &quot;would the beast be able to think tactically&quot;, &quot;would it be able to use mental disciplines&quot; and &quot;what about blood sorcery&quot;, etc. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T16:15:51-07:00","id":1511934,"title":"","content":"I mostly run Sabbat games, so I have very different view of Humanity. In my games, Humanity is effectively a disease that is ultimately fatal if not cured. Humanity makes one miserable, even suicidal, if it is effectively maintained. Moreover, it is almost impossible to maintain, and the doomed attempt will ultimately result in you becoming a wight. So Humanity is a lose-lose proposition. Maintain it and become suicidal, attempt to maintain it but fail and become a wight.<br /> <br /> I generally emphasize this by having Cammies look like late-stage cancer victims when my players infiltrate Camarilla cities. Camarilla neonates especially are miserable creatures. They are generally very close to becoming wights because their cowardly elders make them do all the dirty work, but don't teach them the Paths of Enlightenment needed to avoid wightdom when routinely doing that dirty work.<br /> <br /> This really drives home the importance of converting Cammy neonates to the Sabbat, and thereby saving them from the cancer of Humanity.<br /> <br /> In the context of your original question, that means I treat Humanity very harshly. It's largely implicit because the players either start without Humanity, or only have it during the period when they are training in a Path of Enlightenment, so Humanity is largely an NPC thing in my games. But the very fact that most Cammy NPCs are very close to becoming wights implicitly means Humanity is extremely hard to maintain while doing the sort of things a vampire needs to do to survive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3598,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","user":"CajunKhan","date":". 05-17-2023, 08:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T17:05:36-07:00","id":1511938,"title":"","content":"Homebrew (without any sort of playtesting whatsoever)!<br /> <br /> No Humanity/Road/Path ratings. You instead have three <b>Virtues</b> rated 1 to 5; Courage, Prudence, Temperance. When you do something shitty that runs afoul of one of those, or basic human decency as your human self would\u2019ve judged, roll a dice pool made of that Virtue plus another of your choice (for the latter, two of your best). Success means you walk away this time. Failure means that you end up with something like a Stain, or a Flaw, etc. That\u2019s basic, default Humanity. A <b>Road of Humanity</b> empoweres one of your Virtues and gives you \u201cDo\u201d tenets that provide bonuses if your shitty act was made in their pursuit or otherwise reinforces them. A <b>Path of Enlightenment</b> erases one of your Virtues and saddles you with \u201cDo Not\u201d tenets (that you now only have two Virtues to roll against), but saves you from everything that don\u2019t expressly and grossly go against those tenets, and upon initiation also sears away all prior accumulated Flaws/Stains/etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T21:21:07-07:00","id":1511969,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mostly run Sabbat games, so I have very different view of Humanity. In my games, Humanity is effectively a disease that is ultimately fatal if not cured. Humanity makes one miserable, even suicidal, if it is effectively maintained. Moreover, it is almost impossible to maintain, and the doomed attempt will ultimately result in you becoming a wight. So Humanity is a lose-lose proposition. Maintain it and become suicidal, attempt to maintain it but fail and become a wight.<br /> \u200b </div> </div> </div>It's an interesting idea, I got to say. A completely different point of view and I quite enjoyed someone saying &quot;Humanity is a disease&quot; to a vampire, in the sense that the Vampire thinking he could still act as a normal human is a kind of disease, very thought-provoking I'd say.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No Humanity/Road/Path ratings. You instead have three <b>Virtues</b> rated 1 to 5; Courage, Prudence, Temperance. When you do something shitty that runs afoul of one of those, or basic human decency as your human self would\u2019ve judged, roll a dice pool made of that Virtue plus another of your choice (for the latter, two of your best). Success means you walk away this time. Failure means that you end up with something like a Stain, or a Flaw, etc. That\u2019s basic, default Humanity. A <b>Road of Humanity</b> empoweres one of your Virtues and gives you \u201cDo\u201d tenets that provide bonuses if your shitty act was made in their pursuit or otherwise reinforces them. A <b>Path of Enlightenment</b> erases one of your Virtues and saddles you with \u201cDo Not\u201d tenets (that you now only have two Virtues to roll against), but saves you from everything that don\u2019t expressly and grossly go against those tenets, and upon initiation also sears away all prior accumulated Flaws/Stains/etc.\u200b\u200b </div> </div> </div>Giving flaws/stain, etc, to Cainites who fail their tests seem like a cool idea, but it's too experimental and I like the way the paths work, so I'm not very interested in going for radical changes, the thread was more to understand how other STs deal with the question.<br /> <br /> Talking about that, do you guys think dlablerie is always a sin?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12348,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","user":"Herr Meister","date":". 05-18-2023, 02:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T23:28:50-07:00","id":1511978,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511904-what-s-your-vision-of-humanity?p=1511933#post1511933\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You brought up two other interesting ideas, one is the question of the Beast being &quot;bad&quot;, the way I see it, the answer would most likely be &quot;yes&quot;, it only cares for itself and is a destructive force altogether. Another point if how &quot;intelligent&quot; is the Beast, this is something that often becomes the subject of discussion with questions like: &quot;would the beast be able to think tactically&quot;, &quot;would it be able to use mental disciplines&quot; and &quot;what about blood sorcery&quot;, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>Worth to mention - if you don't use the Beast as an unawakened P'o or a Shadow from Wraith - the Beast is quite... passive.<br /> It's an aspect of the soul, the mind, the psyche. It's the id. The primal urges and instincts of every animal that wants to survive and thrive.<br /> Every living thing of sufficient complexity has a Beast - vampires and werewolves are just the only beings to have a &quot;supernaturally enhanced&quot; Beast. - witch loops back to my Wraith/Shadow example (the P'o is really just the Shadow)<br /> This is pretty much explicitly stated in the rules text for Quell the Beast (Animalism 3). It says that the vampire cows their target's inner Beast, and then immediately goes on to describe the power's effect on mortals. It makes them lethargic and apathetic, acting only to stay alive.<br /> Scry the Soul (Auspex 3) also explicitly states that it can detect whether or not someone has a <i>supernatural</i> Beast, which would mark them as either a vampire or werewolf.<br /> Everyone has a Beast, which is why vampires are stated to experience it differently - sometimes as a set of urges and instincts, sometimes as an actual voice in their head. No two vampires' Beasts are exactly alike. The Beast is personal. The Beast is You.<br /> \u200b<br /> In Mage:the Ascension, it's explained that when a human becomes a Vampire, he loses his Avatar (and therefore his ability to use magic).<br /> This may indicate that &quot;the Beast&quot; is the animalish side of a human, stripped off everything that makes us humans (not only intelligence).<br /> So the Beast is a bestial human, but at a lower level than an animal- The Beast is the mask vampires use to excuse giving into their hunger if you will.<br /> <br /> There are Flaws (and even Merits I think) that basically let you to give sentinence and awareness (even intelligence) to your Beast, making it more powerfull, but more dangerous to you.<br /> <br /> For example, in Players Gudie to High Clans there is:<br /> <br /> <b>Gifts of the Beast (Variable Cost Supernatural Merit)</b><br /> Your Beast has a terrible awareness and power all its own that emerges when its wrath or fear overtakes you. You may select additional Traits that manifest only during frenzy or R\u00f6tschreck, as approved by the Storyteller. Such Beast Traits cost two fewer bonus points than normal, to a minimum cost of 1, and must be instinctive or physical in nature. In the case of Disciplines, only physical Disciplines are permitted (Celerity, Fortitude or Potence), even if they are not normally associated with your clan. Beast Traits are cumulative with normal traits, although they cannot exceed generational limits. Additional Beast Traits may not be gained or increased with experience. For example, the Toreador Justinius is normally quiet and re- served. When his slumbering Beast awakens, however, its speed and killing prowess possess him.<br /> He gains an additional dot of Celerity and Dexterity and three dots of Brawl. The total cost of the Merit is 10 bonus points (7 \u2013 2 for the Celerity, 5 \u2013 2 for the Dexterity, and 2 \u2013 2, minimum 1 for each dot of Brawl).<br /> <br /> This actually a bit similar how you can costumize your P'o in Kindred of the East <br /> \u200b<br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T23:47:43-07:00","id":1511979,"title":"","content":"When I'm the one doing the storytelling Humanity is not about morality or acting like an actual human being. It is, like all paths, a philosophy of self-discipline meant to keep the Beast from consuming you. Humanity in particular does this through denial; the Beast prioritises preserving a vampire's existence with no regard for the wellbeing of others, so Humanity focusues on the opposite and prioritises the wellbeing of others over oneself.<br /> <br /> Again, morality is not part of this. Dangerous criminals, people you consider evil or deserving of harm, other vampires, the absolute scum of the Earth; all will trigger a conscience roll for murdering and robbing them. One with mitigated difficulty, perhaps, but a roll nonetheless.<br /> <br /> Nor must it be about humans. It's possible for a high humanity vampire to deem vampires more important than mortals - as mortals do with themselves and animals - and prioritise the wellbeing of their kindred over the kine, who they approach with more of an animal welfare mindset (animal cruelty can also trigger rolls). In this case the name humanity is thought to refer to emulating humans as cooperative, group-forming social animals rather than acting as the asocial solitary predator vampires are by nature.<br /> <br /> <br /> The sins of &quot;violation&quot; refer to manipulating others to serve your wants and needs at the expense of theirs, as well as killing.<br /> (So; ghouling, blackmail, gaslighting, embracing a childe without consent, feeding without knowledge and consent, most uses of disciplines such as dominate and presence and things like that)<br /> <br /> Accidental violation can refer to performing these acts as the lesser evil, where you didn't come out here today intending to do this, but it ended up happening. <br /> <br /> In play, humanity goes like this:<br /> Step 1) Player violates the Path of Humanity<br /> <br /> Step 2) I observe how the player responds in character, emotionally and in reaction. If they don't show remorse or make any attempts at amends then I just remove humanity automatically. If they do move in to the next step.<br /> <br /> Step 3) Difficulty is judged by severity of damage done, the circumstances and their response to the violation. If the action taken was the lesser evil available then the roll is less harsh. Simply being upset but nothing more means a higher difficulty than actively trying to repair the damage done.*<br /> <br /> Step 4) Roll conscience. I don't tell players the result or what their humanity is, I simply give them a feel for how close wassail is through narration and frenzy rolls, which became more frequent and harder to reign in as humanity drops**.<br /> <br /> * If the player tries to make amends at a later point in the game I will reroll with appropriate difficulty. If they succeed I restore the lost humanity.<br /> <br /> ** Homerule: the difficulty for frenzy rolls is your path rating. So at humanity 10 you'd only frenzy if you botched, at humanity 1 you need 10s to succeed.<br /> Frequency is just a matter of judgement. If you light a cigarette in front of a humanity 10-6 vamp they'll just feel uncomfortable and avert their gaze. 5-4 are usually fine, but if it's been a really bad night I'll call for a roll. A vampire between 3-1 will flip their shit.<br /> <br /> (I'm considering keeping a &quot;path xp pool&quot; where acting in accordance with the hierarchy nets you xp to spend on your path rating.)<br /> <br /> That said, the final sin confuses me. You can already unthinkingly murder, rape and pillage as many people in the world you want on levels mental and physical to your hearts content and consider it simply another Tuesday night; what the hell else is there to do??","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":26836,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","user":"Rhywbeth","date":". 05-18-2023, 02:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-18T05:29:28-07:00","id":1512002,"title":"","content":"Humanity doesn't make complete sense to me because so many people, and it would go double for vampires, are warped. <br /> <br /> Being indoctrinated into the idea that homosexuality is a sin and having homosexual thoughts would get your conscience rolling, while the extreme mental gymnastics practiced by some child molesters as them think it's fine or even a good thing. Let's not even get into narcissists which just think different and every other flavour of moral impairment. What happens when a truly psychopathic person gets embraced? Someone who sees morality as a foreign code to adapt to in order to hide themselves rather than something intrinsic to them? <br /> <br /> <br /> Personally, I run with the idea that all vampires have Conscience AND Conviction, giving them up to 10 dice to fight degradation, though depending on context you might roll one or the other or even give conscience up if you're on a nasty path (or maybe for certain flaws) Conviction has tenets you choose. <br /> <br /> <br /> Malkavians should have a license to give themselves a faulty morality system as a flaw. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-18T12:24:19-07:00","id":1512035,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1511904-what-s-your-vision-of-humanity?p=1512002#post1512002\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Humanity doesn't make complete sense to me because so many people, and it would go double for vampires, are warped.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be warped requires there to be a definitive baseline that is agreed upon as non-warped. Humanity overall has never had such as thing, hence why all the different cultures tend to bicker with each other about right and wrong. Using your mention of MAPs as an example, the age of consent varies by state and country. Biology tells us that animals whose reproductive systems finish developing start using them regardless of the animal's age. Conversely Biology also tells us that humans don't finish mentally developing until almost the age of 30, but that would mean that before the 1800s most humans raised entire families before &quot;maturing&quot;.<br /> <br /> A better question would be of what ones environment requires of you to survive and thrive. If that just requires the mindset of good manners and living by the notion that one should not bug thy neighbor, then smiles all around. However, if your environment requires you to fight to the death on a semi-regular basis and be very careful about making enemies, then a bit of paranoia and ruthlessness is seen as common sense.<br /> <br /> To a trust fund baby, working a 17 hour a day job without food breaks (you eat while working) and no overtime pay is tantamount to slavery. To an immigrant, those sorts of working conditions are a godsend because no one is shooting at them, there is no food scarcity, and most people tend to be relatively polite when it comes to religious differences.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}